 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Reading a timetable: find the day, then the time. The lesson next to that time is the answer.\nReading a notice: a notice tells who it is for, what will happen, when, where, and what to bring. The person who wrote it signs at the bottom.\nReading an account: an account tells what happened, in order. Look for time words such as first, then, at ten o'clock, in the end.\nAccounts of past events use the past tense. Many common verbs are irregular: they do not add -ed. Learn them:\ngo → went, come → came, take → took, eat → ate, bring → brought, see → saw, give → gave, buy → bought, write → wrote, sit → sat, run → ran, cut → cut.\n“We came at eight o'clock.” (Not: comed.)",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "text": "Last Saturday, the Form One pupils cleaned the school compound. They came at eight o'clock. Some pupils cut the grass, others swept the classrooms, and Tabe and Bih carried the rubbish to the pit. At ten o'clock, the Principal gave everyone bread and a drink. The pupils went home at noon, tired but happy."
  },
  "problem": "Read the account, then answer: what did the Principal do at ten o'clock?\n{text}",
  "steps": [
   "Look for the time words “ten o'clock” in the account.",
   "Read the sentence that holds them: “At ten o'clock, the Principal gave everyone bread and a drink.”",
   "“Gave” is the past of the irregular verb give."
  ],
  "answer": "The Principal gave everyone bread and a drink.",
  "checks": [
   "past('give') == 'gave'"
  ]
 },
